            <h1>reading reflections</h1>
            <p>
                Susans sontangs <em>In Platos Cave</em> from <em>on Photography </em> made me think differently about how ofetn image are taken and how much power they actually have .Sontag explains that photography is not just about capturing moments, but about shaping how we see the world. Even though this text was written before smartphones and social media, it still feels very relevant today because images are everywhere and constantly being created and shared. Photography has become a normal part of daily life, but Sontag reminds us that this normality does not make it harmless or neutral.
                When I take photos or create graphics, I usually think of them as memories, documentation, or creative expression. I photograph moments I want to remember or things I find visually interesting. After reading Sontag, I realize that taking a photo is also a way of choosing what matters. The camera frames reality and leaves other parts out. This means image making can influence how people understand events, emotions, and even other people. A photo can make something seem more important, more dramatic, or more distant than it really is.
                Throughout this semester, the projects in this course feel connected because they all involve making choices about images and communication. Even though each project uses different tools, they are all part of a larger body of work that reflects how I see and interpret the world. As a media maker, I am learning how to build skills gradually and become more intentional with each project. One project informs the next by helping me understand composition, storytelling, and visual meaning more clearly.
                At this moment in the world, photography and image making can represent themes like isolation, connection, overload, and identity. Images today often show crowded digital spaces, constant movement, and emotional intensity. Through photography or illustration, these ideas could be shown using contrasts, such as light and shadow, close-ups versus wide shots, or the combination of images with text. Adding text can guide interpretation and make viewers slow down instead of scrolling past the image. 
                Overall, Sontag’s writing encourages me to be more aware of the responsibility that comes with image making. Photography is not just about recording reality, but about shaping how reality is understood. As I continue creating images and graphics this semester, I want to think more critically about what my work communicates and how it fits into the larger visual culture around us.
                
